Df Refill at home strategy is used to design packages and services that enable customers to refill their reusable packages at home. Customers pay for the content and in some cases for the containers, dispensers, and delivery. Two key dimensions that define this strategy are reuse enabling behaviour (refill), and location where that behaviour occurs (home). This strategy can be used as a part of circular business models and PSSs to effectively achieve economic and environmental benefits. It can help companies reduce packaging and transportation costs as the refills are lighter in weight. Recyclable plastic pouches, refill pods, refill inserts, and bulk refill packages are some examples of refill packages for refill at-home solutions. 
Certain principles and guidelines exist to design packaging and product service systems utilising Df Refill at home strategy such as:
• Designing packages in a way that it is easy to refill the reusable container multiple times.
• Considering the challenges that users face during use such as convenience, hygiene, messy refilling experiences, and behaviour change barriers while designing the packaging and the product-service systems.
• Considering the end-of-life of both reusable packaging and single-use refills and designing and manufacturing the product to be recyclable, compostable, or biodegradable.